      <h1>Cut Your Electricity Bill — Free Solar &amp; Battery Calculator</h1>
      <p class="lede">See how solar and batteries can cut your electricity bill using NASA weather and honest payback math. Free, no signup, nothing for sale.</p>
      <section id="sizing" class="calculator">
        <h2>Size Your System</h2>
        <form id="sizing-form" action="#sizing" method="get">
          <fieldset>
            <legend>Your location and load</legend>
            <div class="field-group">
              <label for="postcode">Postcode or city<span class="required-star" aria-hidden="true">*</span></label>
              <input type="text" id="postcode" name="postcode" autocomplete="address-level2" placeholder="e.g. London, UK or 90210" />
              <p class="field-hint">We only use this to pull NASA weather for your exact spot. Nothing leaves your browser.</p>
            </div>
            <fieldset>
              <legend>What do you use in a year?</legend>
              <div class="field-group">
                <label for="annual-kwh">Annual electricity use (kWh)<span class="required-star" aria-hidden="true">*</span></label>
                <input type="number" id="annual-kwh" name="annual-kwh" min="0" step="100" />
                <p class="field-hint">Your bill says kWh per year — not dollars. If you only know dollars, drop your tariff in the next box and we will back into it.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="field-group">
                <label for="tariff">Your electricity tariff ($/kWh)</label>
                <input type="number" id="tariff" name="tariff" min="0" step="0.001" />
                <p class="field-hint">Optional. Use it to size a system against your actual bill, or leave it and we size against your kWh only.</p>
              </div>
            </fieldset>
          </fieldset>
          <fieldset>
            <legend>How off-grid do you want to be?</legend>
            <div class="radio-group">
              <label class="radio"><input type="radio" name="grid-mode" value="off-grid-basic" checked />
                <span><strong>Off-grid, basic resilience</strong> — sized to cover ~70% of hours from your own solar+battery. Short dark spells still draw from the grid; biggest bang-for-buck cut of the bill.</span>
              </label>
              <label class="radio"><input type="radio" name="grid-mode" value="off-grid-strong" />
                <span><strong>Off-grid, strong resilience</strong> — sized to cover ~90% of hours from your own system. You still buy from the grid on the worst months, but most of the year is self-powered.</span>
              </label>
              <label class="radio"><input type="radio" name="grid-mode" value="off-grid-full" />
                <span><strong>Off-grid, full independence</strong> — sized to cover ~99% of hours from your own system. Comfortable most years, with seasonal overbuild. This is the expensive end — the calculator tells you honestly when the last few percent cost more than they are worth.</span>
              </label>
              <label class="radio"><input type="radio" name="grid-mode" value="grid-tie" />
                <span><strong>Grid-tied bill-cutter</strong> — not trying to go off-grid at all. Sized to chip away at the bill with a grid-tied solar system sized against your tariff, with no battery unless you ask for one to cut peaks.</span>
              </label>
            </div>
          </fieldset>
          <fieldset>
            <legend>Backup battery? (optional)</legend>
            <div class="field-group">
              <label for="battery-hours">How many hours of house load to back up<span class="required-star" aria-hidden="true">*</span></label>
              <input type="number" id="battery-hours" name="battery-hours" min="0" step="0.5" placeholder="e.g. 8 for a day, 24 for a day of autonomy" />
              <p class="field-hint">How long you want to keep the essentials going without sun or grid. We size the bank against that, not your whole year.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="field-group">
              <label for="battery-tech">Battery chemistry</label>
              <select id="battery-tech" name="battery-tech">
                <option value="lifepo4">Lithium iron phosphate (LFP) — the current workhorse</option>
                <option value="sodium">Sodium-ion — emerging, cheaper at scale, different durability story</option>
                <option value="lead-acid">Lead-acid — still real for surge duty and temperate climates</option>
              </select>
              <p class="field-hint">Each one has a different honest cost and lifetime. We show them side by side so you can compare, not pick a winner for you.</p>
            </div>
          </fieldset>
          <button type="submit" class="primary-btn">Size my system</button>
        </form>
        <section id="sizing-results" class="results" aria-live="polite" hidden>
          <h2 class="results-heading">Your system, sized</h2>
          <div class="result-grid">
            <div class="result-card">
              <h3>Solar array</h3>
              <p class="result-value" id="result-solar-kw">—</p>
              <p class="result-unit">peak kilowatts</p>
            </div>
            <div class="result-card">
              <h3>Battery bank</h3>
              <p class="result-value" id="result-battery-kwh">—</p>
              <p class="result-unit">usable kWh</p>
            </div>
            <div class="result-card">
              <h3>Bill cut</h3>
              <p class="result-value" id="result-bill-cut">—</p>
              <p class="result-unit">per year, from your tariff</p>
            </div>
            <div class="result-card">
              <h3>Payback</h3>
              <p class="result-value" id="result-payback">—</p>
              <p class="result-unit">years, honest hardware cost</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="results-note">These are honest hardware costs for the cells and power hardware you actually need — not an installer quote. Real installations cost more because someone has to mount, wire, permit, and stand behind it. The calculator's job is to show you the floor so you can tell when an installer quote is carrying a markup, not to sell you anything.</p>
          <section class="results-breakdown" aria-label="How the numbers break down">
            <h3>How the numbers break down</h3>
            <ul id="results-list" class="breakdown-list">
              <li><span class="breakdown-label" id="breakdown-solar-cost">—</span></li>
              <li><span class="breakdown-label" id="breakdown-battery-cost">—</span></li>
              <li><span class="breakdown-label" id="breakdown-inverter-cost">—</span></li>
              <li><span class="breakdown-label" id="breakdown-lcoe">—</span></li>
            </ul>
          </section>
          <section class="results-sensitivity" aria-label="What changes the answer">
            <h3>What changes the answer</h3>
            <p class="sensitivity-copy">Move your postcode a few miles, change your tariff by a cent, or switch battery chemistry and the answer moves — that is the point. The calculator is showing you the range, not a single number to paste into a grant application.</p>
            <ul class="sensitivity-list">
              <li><span class="sensitivity-label">Solar on your actual roof</span> — shading, roof pitch, and which way it faces change the harvest more than the hardware choice does.</li>
              <li><span class="sensitivity-label">Your real bill, not your real kWh</span> — if your tariff changes next year, the payback math changes with it.</li>
              <li><span class="sensitivity-label">Battery depth of discharge</span> — the usable fraction of the bank is what matters, not the nameplate.</li>
            </ul>
          </section>
        </section>
      </section>
      <section id="heatmap" class="heatmap-section">
        <h2>Payback Heatmap — where solar pays fastest</h2>
        <p class="heatmap-lede">Drag this map around or search a city to see how payback changes across the world. Darker red means a shorter honest payback on a representative off-grid basic system — not a promise, just the shape of the opportunity.</p>
        <div class="heatmap-wrap">
          <canvas id="heatmap-canvas" class="heatmap-canvas" role="img" aria-label="World payback heatmap, darker red means shorter payback"></canvas>
          <div class="heatmap-controls">
            <input type="text" id="heatmap-search" class="heatmap-search" placeholder="Search a city — e.g. Honolulu, Nairobi, Berlin" />
            <div class="heatmap-legend" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="legend-label">Fastest payback</span>
              <i class="legend-swatch legend-swatch-fast"></i>
              <i class="legend-swatch legend-swatch-mid"></i>
              <i class="legend-swatch legend-swatch-slow"></i>
              <span class="legend-label">Slowest payback</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="heatmap-note">This is a representative model at a single point in time, not a forecast. Tariff changes, hardware prices, and your own roof can move a specific location in or out of this picture by years — treat the map as a scout, not a verdict.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="bom" class="bom-section">
        <h2>Hardware Reference — what is actually in the box</h2>
        <p class="bom-lede">A plain-English inventory of the pieces the calculator sizes, with honest price bands and the tradeoffs that matter. Not an installer catalog — a reference so you can recognize when a quote is carrying markup.</p>
        <ul class="bom-list">
          <li>
            <span class="bom-term">Solar panels (modules)</span>
            <span class="bom-def">The power source. Rated in peak watts under standard test conditions; real harvest is lower because real weather is real. The dominant cost in cheap systems is the panels, but the dominant cost in expensive systems is the labor to mount them well.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="bom-term">Charge controller (MPPT/PWM)</span>
            <span class="bom-def">Regulates the panel output into the battery. MPPT extracts more harvest in mismatched or cold conditions; PWM is cheaper and adequate when panel voltage and battery voltage are already close.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="bom-term">Inverter / battery inverter</span>
            <span class="bom-def">Turns DC from the battery into AC for your house, or in a grid-tied design, exports surplus to the grid. Size it to your peak load, not your daily kWh, or you stall under surges.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="bom-term">Battery bank</span>
            <span class="bom-def">Stores energy for dark hours. Usable capacity — not nameplate — is the number that matters, and usable capacity depends on chemistry and how deep you let it cycle.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="bom-term">Balance-of-system hardware</span>
            <span class="bom-def">Enclosure, cabling, overcurrent protection, disconnects, grounding. The unsexy part that determines whether the system actually survives. The calculator sizes the power pieces; you or an installer still need this.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
